Continue the same brief: a home baker displaying a small cake menu on narrow phones and larger screens. In this lesson, turn the plan into working material. Keep the intended audience in mind and make one deliberate choice at a time, so you can explain why your result is useful rather than relying on a template or an automatic feature.

Sample inputs: fictional menu cards for vanilla loaf, chocolate cupcakes and coconut slices; long sample label “Celebration-size chocolate cupcakes”. These are fictional practice details; replace them only with material you own or have permission to use.

Exercise: Use Grid or Flexbox to arrange cards when space permits. A grid with repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr)) is one possible starting point; understand each part before copying it. Keep a sensible maximum content width and center the container with available space. Add a media query only where the content needs a different arrangement, rather than targeting one phone brand. Let text wrap naturally and inspect long names. Style links or buttons with visible keyboard focus and enough room around their labels.

Runnable CSS starter (apply to a container with class menu and cards inside):
* { box-sizing: border-box; }
body { margin: 0; font: 1rem/1.5 sans-serif; padding: 1rem; }
.menu { display: grid; grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(min(100%, 15rem), 1fr)); gap: 1rem; max-width: 65rem; margin-inline: auto; }
.menu > article { padding: 1rem; border: 1px solid #777; }
img { max-width: 100%; height: auto; }
a:focus-visible, button:focus-visible { outline: 3px solid #174ea6; outline-offset: 3px; }
Try three article cards; resize the browser and inspect the long cupcake name.

Review checklist: Card widths adapt and the long product label does not overlap other content. Write down one observation before making a correction, then compare the revised result with the previous version.

Common mistake: Adding a separate fixed-width rule for every device.

Save your evidence: keep this stage of the work so you can explain your choices in the final mini-task. This is original Learning Hub practice, not a vendor course or certificate. The linked official resource provides supporting guidance; software labels and availability may vary by version.

Continue the same brief: a market cooperative learning how a small JavaScript function totals produce orders. In this lesson, turn the plan into working material. Keep the intended audience in mind and make one deliberate choice at a time, so you can explain why your result is useful rather than relying on a template or an automatic feature.

Sample inputs: sample prices [1200, 800, 500], expected total 2500, an empty list expected to total 0, and a separate invalid-input example. These are fictional practice details; replace them only with material you own or have permission to use.

Exercise: Write function sumPrices(prices) with a total initialized to zero, a loop over the prices and a returned total. Run the sample, then trace how total changes from 0 to 1200, 2000 and 2500. Explain why the initial value matters for an empty list. Add a simple assertion for the example and one for []. Show what happens when a string appears in the input, then explain whether you will reject invalid values or explicitly limit the demonstration to numeric arrays. Do not silently hide an unexpected result.

Runnable JavaScript (paste into a local console):
function sumPrices(prices) {
  if (!Array.isArray(prices) || prices.some(p => typeof p !== 'number' || !Number.isFinite(p) || p < 0)) throw new TypeError('Use nonnegative numeric prices');
  let total = 0;
  for (const price of prices) total += price;
  return total;
}
console.assert(sumPrices([1200, 800, 500]) === 2500);
console.assert(sumPrices([]) === 0);
console.assert(sumPrices([300, 700]) === 1000);
try { sumPrices([300, '700']); } catch (error) { console.log(error.message); }
This teaching example is not a complete money-handling library. Explain the input guard and the empty-list test.

Review checklist: A trace connects each iteration to the final total; empty input is handled. Write down one observation before making a correction, then compare the revised result with the previous version.

Common mistake: Using a large application that distracts from the concept.

